From 073c21317d87f9e98993bd9067fa8fdf0229e922 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:03:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH pve-qemu-kyber] kyber-qemu-server: read DMA-BUF scanouts back
 through EGL (NVIDIA)

The zero-copy path maps the scanout into a VAAPI surface and encodes
there.  NVIDIA has no such VAAPI: nvidia-vaapi-driver only decodes, and
the RGB frames context fails on the first frame ("Failed to create
surface: 14 (the requested RT Format is not supported)"), so a
virtio-gl guest streams nothing.  The buffer is block-linear, so the
mmap fallback cannot read it either.

--dmabuf-readback still requests DMA-BUF scanouts, but imports each
buffer with EGL (GBM platform, surfaceless context, the modifier
passed), blits it into a linear framebuffer honouring y0_top and reads
it with glReadPixels into a BGRA surface for the software path, which
encodes with --encoder (h264_nvenc on NVIDIA).  A buffer is imported
once (keyed by its inode, as QEMU sends fresh fds for the same buffer)
and read only when QEMU reported damage.

Tested on an RTX 3080 (driver 595.91, QEMU 11.0, virtio-vga-gl guest on
a private per-VM bus, 1280x800, a terminal running top -d 0.1): 20 s,
h264_nvenc, 1226 frames encoded, 0 dropped, 581 scanouts and 579
updates from the guest, the picture correct (orientation, colours);
about 24 % of one CPU core with h264_nvenc, 36 % with libx264.  The
--dmabuf (VAAPI) path is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Dmitry R <rdmitry0911@gmail.com>
